A||||24||86SE||Does the Fiero have Posi-traction?||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||01-22-2000||01:55 AM||||I have a 86 SE auto and when I floor it it leaves 2 strips of black rubber on the ground, does the fiero have posi-track?? what in the heck is it any way??||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||01-22-2000||03:49 AM||||This is just an uninformed guess from someone who knows less about cars than the Pope.  I'd have to say yes and no.  (How's that for straddling the fence?!)  I think there's power to both rear wheels, but I don't think it could really be called a posi.  If I remember correctly (I usually don't) the Posi diff didn't slip meaning if you could (theoretically) make a true 90deg corner one tire would slide.  I think the Fiero (since it's based on FWD stuff) would have to be more like a limited slip.  I hope this makes sense.  It's late and I'm tired.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-22-2000||02:17 PM||||TH125 autotranny is not available with "posi" on any car as far as I'm aware. If it ever was an option it's got to be a rare one.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||01-22-2000||03:07 PM||||Posi and limited slip are pretty similar in action, when one wheel spins a predetermined amount faster than the other a friction device (clutch) puts power to the slower wheel to bring it up to speed.  A locked differential is just that, both wheels run the same speed all the time, shearing rubber off the tires every time the car is turned.  Aftermarket suppliers have the components to do either to the Fiero, but the car is so finely tuned it doesn't seem necessary for everyday driving.  Oops, hold that presumptuous thought of mine.  Knowing for sure that the rear end will pop out when the wheels spin can be very important for reliably sideways driving.  Guess I'm just getting old and incoherent.  Homocidal tendencies be dam*ed, I will NOT take my meds.||||reg||1
Z||000004||86SE||01-22-2000||03:51 PM||||About a month ago I was driving and accelerating up an icey hill and my back end swayed way to the right, i recoved then it swayed way to the left and my left front tire hit a rail road track and swung the back end of my car hard into the ditch, i almost rolled it!!  it waw parked in the ditch at about a 55-65 degree angle.........||||reg||8
Z||000005||Monkeyman||01-22-2000||04:07 PM||||Welcome to winter! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||01-22-2000||04:28 PM||||I would agree with Ogre....  Find out the old fasion way.  Do a burnout and check to see if you have one or two tire marks.  hehe.<BR>or jack up the rear-end and turn the passenger side wheel CLOCKWISE.  If the other wheel doesn't turn, or it turns the opposite direction, you haven't got a posi... They must both turn the same direction.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||01-22-2000||06:02 PM||||It is my understanding that all GM FWD cars have a limited slip differential to help reduce torque steer.This started with the Toronado,and since the Fiero uses FWD components,it's safe to assume that it does too.The percentage,or slip will vary due to use/wear,and type of lube used.The original GM limited slip additive was made from certain whale by-products and has been revised,or discontinued since the late seventies or early eighties.If the car lays two stripes that's a pretty good determination I'd say.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||01-22-2000||06:15 PM||||If you look at the diagrams of the differential in the 22P manual you'll see no clutch pack.  There was talk a couple of years ago on the list from someone who machined out clearance in his gearcase to put a limited slip clutchpack in his Fiero.<P>I'm pretty sure no Fiero was offered with limited slip or "positraction".||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||01-22-2000||07:09 PM||||Two stripes and no real mechanism for slip?<BR>Not even one Belleville washer against something else?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cooter||01-22-2000||08:36 PM||||Let me try this- In a front engine, rear drive, the engine torque causes more power to be twisted to the right tire. So with an open rear end, it usually only lays down one black mark. With our engine layout, engine torque doesnt load one drive tire more than the other, so it lays down rubber with both tires.||||reg||1
Z||000011||cobra427||01-22-2000||08:51 PM||||cooter, you are absolutely correct.Its all a matter of transverse vs. non transverse and the way the engines react in their positions in the engine bay-longtitudinally or transverse.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||01-23-2000||03:34 AM||||So what's the answer already?!  Do we have Posi-traction, limited slip or an open diff?||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||01-23-2000||09:17 AM||||It's not a true "posi" otherwise known as limited slip, but it acts like it because of the drivetrain configuration and weight distribution.||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||01-23-2000||09:45 AM||||The only true "Posi" out there is a locked spool,all others will have some degree of slip,otherwise the rear of the car would wiggle all over in tight turns as the faster moving outer wheel will fight with the slower moving inner wheel for traction.<BR>A clutch of some sort is required to strike a streetable balance between driveability and performance,and is often an inexpensive steel washer that loses it's bite after a few thousand miles.The slip/bite of these clutches isn't always consistent and can be felt under very hard excelleration,pitching the car back and forth as one wheel applies power,then two,and back and forth.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||01-23-2000||12:15 PM||||If you want to feel some serious wheel hop with a Posi find a friend with a big truck and ask him to take you around some tight corners with the power divider (differential lock) on.  Whew!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Carrolles||01-23-2000||01:27 PM||||There is no limited slip mechanism in the Fiero transaxels.  We are just fortunate that they behave well.||||reg||1
Z||000017||olympic||01-23-2000||01:28 PM||||   My brother welded up the spider gears in his 76 Camaro (they were shot anyway). It was great for burning donuts on the hiway and for racing in a quarter mile but it didn't like to turn. He actually ran the car off the road and into a dugout because he couldn't make a turn.  I'll never forget that car because my bro put a 455 rocket in it from a 1970 Olds Delta 88. I'm think that engine was rated at 375 Hp from the factory. Then he installed a huge racing cam and a hirise intake, headers, and zoomies(4 exhaust pipes out each side of the car.) The whole car would rock back and forth at idle and it would pin you back in the seat all the way past 120 mph. The damn thing would't even run smoothly until 80 or 90 mph.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||01-23-2000||03:20 PM||||Probably 375horses at the flywheel not to the ground.  They rated hp differently back then.  But I'll bet it had TONS of torque!!||||reg||1
Z||000019||geyser||01-23-2000||04:35 PM||||Are you sure one of the strips insn't oil?||||reg||1
Z||000020||geyser||01-23-2000||04:55 PM||||"Positraction" is the marketing name for limited slip differentials by General Motors, if anyone really cares. All the manufacturers had names for their  brands,"Power Lock", "Detroit Locker" etc..  Ford's was probably the most true locking differential. Fiero did not get one, it did'nt need it.||||reg||1
